              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "Despite his name, **Flounder is not actually a flounder** (which is a flat, brown, bottom-dwelling fish with both eyes on one side of its head). \n\nThe actual kind of fish he is depends on which version of *The Little Mermaid* you are looking at:\n\n### 1. In the 2023 Live-Action Film\nIn the live-action remake, Flounder's species is officially identified as a **sergeant major** (*Abudefduf saxatilis*). Sergeant majors are a type of tropical damselfish known for their yellow bodies and dark blue-to-black vertical stripes. This realistic, slimmer look sparked a lot of memes and discussion online upon the film's release.\n\n### 2. In the Original 1989 Animated Film\nIn the animated classic, Flounder is simply a fictional, brightly colored **tropical fish**. The animators did not base him on one specific real-life species, but rather designed him to look cute, chubby, and vibrant. However, he has two primary real-world inspirations:\n* **Blue Angelfish:** Marine biologists and fans agree that his round body shape, bright yellow coloring, and vibrant blue stripes most closely resemble a blue angelfish.\n* **Guppy:** In the *Little Mermaid* animated television spin-off, characters occasionally refer to him as a guppy (and Ariel frequently uses \"guppy\" as a fond nickname for him).",
